Data-flow patterns that turned out to be right in more than one place. Proven
useful, therefore worth reaching for first the next time something looks similar.

This file starts thin, because the project has not run yet. A pattern earns its
way in here by being right twice.

--

## Generate, then view. Never both at once.

The server produces world state; the browser draws it. The generator produces a
world; the server runs it. The layout stage produces a graph; the realise stage
turns it into geometry. The Markdown is the data; the HTML is a view of it.

Every time this split exists, a bug has a side, and the search space halves. Every
time it does not, a wrong pixel and a wrong rule look identical, and there is no
way to tell them apart except by reading everything.

The split is claimed at four boundaries in this project. It is only real at a
boundary where something has actually been swapped across it, which is why phase
9 builds a second renderer -- a claim that is never tested is a claim that is false
eventually.

## The security answer and the pretty answer are often the same answer.

The visibility polygon exists because the outbound filter must know which records
it may write. It turns out to be exactly what the renderer needs to draw a clean
edge between light and dark.

The general shape: when a constraint forces you to compute something exactly that
you would otherwise have approximated, the exact version is usually better at the
approximate version's job too. Watch for it, because it arrives disguised as luck.

## A small integer beats a measurement.

Indices, not pointers. Ticks, not seconds. Region membership, not distance to the
tavern. Fixed point, not floating point.

Every one of these is the same trade: give up a little expressiveness, get back
something that can be compared exactly, saved without translation, and reproduced
on another machine. The one place this project spends a real-valued measurement is
position, and even there it is an integer wearing a scale factor.

## Zero is a sentinel. Nil is a question.

Index 0 of every array is a reserved empty record. Nothing in the world is ever
null. A nil check asks whether some earlier code did its job, and that question
belongs in a validator that runs once, not in a loop that runs ten thousand times
a tick.

## Buffer, then resolve.

Motion is written as intent and committed in a second pass. Two bodies reaching
for the same doorway both write, and the resolve decides, so the outcome does not
depend on which thread got there first.

Whenever two things must be able to happen at the same instant, write both down
first and settle them afterwards. This is what makes the tick deterministic, and
determinism is what makes a replay mean anything.

## Dispatch tables, not branches.

The tick's passes, the command verbs, the ruleset's hooks, the generator's
stages. In every case the value already knows what it means, and the table says
what knowing that means. Adding a case is adding a row.

The real payoff is not speed. It is that **the order of the simulation became a
piece of readable data** instead of something buried in a function body.

## Refuse loudly, in words.

Every rejected command carries a reason, and the reason is a sentence. This is not
politeness -- it is the only teaching mechanism the program has, because nobody
reads a rules screen. A person finds out that the door is barred, that the goblin
belongs to the forest, that they cannot see around that corner, at the moment they
try.

The same standard applies to every validator: name what was missing and where, and
never substitute a default.

## Make the tool, not the thing.

Maps are generated. The documentation's HTML is generated. The demos are
regenerated. Anything hand-tweaked is a thing nobody dares change afterwards, and
a thing nobody dares change is how a tool dies.

## One door out.

There is exactly one function that may write a thing record to a socket, and it
takes the viewer as an argument. The question "can this leak?" is therefore
answered by reading one function and checking its callers, rather than by reading
the whole program.

Generalises: when a property must hold everywhere, build a single choke point
where it can be checked, and forbid the bypass. A rule enforced in one place is a
rule; a rule enforced in forty places is a habit.
